  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nCURRENT STORM: \n\nA shortwave trough is currently tracking over the area. It has \nresulted in widespread rain and snow showers. Snow levels made it \ndown to about 7 kft this morning and those may slightly rise through \nout the day, especially if there is a break in the \nprecipitation. The southern mountains are still favored to \nreceive an additional 1-4\" of snow with lesser amounts for the \ncentral and northern mountains. Rain amounts have reached 0.50\" \nin spots with another 0.25\" possible. Showers will start to \ndissipate this evening with most places dry by midnight. \nTomorrow and Monday look to be mostly dry with slightly warmer \ntemperatures. \n\n\nNEXT STORM: \n\nAnother shortwave trough very similar to the current is expected to \nimpact the area on Tuesday and Wednesday. We should expect similar \nprecipitation amounts and snow levels (7-9 kft). This system is \nslightly cooler, but the bulk of the precipitation is ahead and \nalong the front. When the cold front arrives on Wednesday most of \nthe showers will be decreasing in coverage. Snowfall amounts are \nmuch like the current system with the highest totals in southern \nmountains around 4-8\" and lesser amounts further north.\n\n\nLATE WEEK STORM:\n\nThe models continue to advertise another storm Friday and Saturday \nalthough the trends are shifting towards an open wave, much like the \nprevious two systems. Due to the model inconsistency confidence is \nnot high at this point, but with an open wave the precipitation \namounts have trended downward. Timing will be something to watch \nwith this wave as it may bring the snow levels down to some of the \nvalleys Friday night into Saturday morning. Although once again the \nbulk of the precipitation is forecasted to occur ahead of the \ncoldest air, so it does not appear the valleys will get much\nattm.",
